# Space Relics - environment variables (copy to .env.local)
#
# Only DATABASE_URL is required for mission storage. Everything else is
# optional and improves production behaviour.

# --- Required: mission persistence -------------------------------------------
# Managed PostgreSQL (Neon), server-only. NEVER prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# In Vercel: Project -> Settings -> Environment Variables.
#   Production -> the production database connection string.
#   Preview    -> a SEPARATE database (a Neon branch of its own). Preview builds
#                 auto-apply pending migrations on their first request, so reusing
#                 the production string lets a preview deployment change live data.
# Example shape:
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST/DB?sslmode=require
# DATABASE_URL=

# --- Recommended: exact origin for mission writes -----------------------------
# The canonical deployment origin, e.g. https://space-relics.vercel.app
# Optional: preview deployments fall back to the request own origin.
# APP_ORIGIN=

# --- Recommended in production: shared rate limiting (Upstash Redis) --------- 
# Without these the API still rate-limits, but per instance only. The fallback
# is reported as degraded in /api/health, never as distributed protection.
# Provision an Upstash Redis database, then add BOTH of these to the Vercel
# Production environment (Project -> Settings -> Environment Variables). For
# Preview use a separate Upstash database so preview traffic cannot consume the
# production rate-limit budget. They are server-only: never use a NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix.
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# --- Optional ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed when the browser must call the API on a different origin.
# Leave empty to use the same-origin /api routes.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=

# Port for next dev / next start (default 3000)
# PORT=

# Only used by npm run db:export-sqlite to find the legacy v1 SQLite file.
# DATABASE_PATH=
